        "content": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n<title>Can You Tell That I'm in a Relationship? Attachment and Relationship Visibility on Facebook</title>\n<script type=\"text/javascript\" id=\"MathJax-script\" async\n  src=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mathjax@3/es5/tex-mml-chtml.js\">\n</script>\n<style>\n  body {\n    font-family: Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;\n    color: #1a1a1a;\n    max-width: 850px;\n    margin: 0 auto;\n    padding: 40px 50px;\n    line-height: 1.4;\n  }\n\n  .article-label {\n    font-size: 12px;\n    letter-spacing: 1px;\n    color: #333;\n    border-bottom: 1px solid #999;\n    padding-bottom: 6px;\n    margin-bottom: 20px;\n  }\n\n  .header-row {\n    display: flex;\n    justify-content: space-between;\n    align-items: flex-start;\n    gap: 20px;\n    border-bottom: 2px solid #000;\n    padding-bottom: 20px;\n    margin-bottom: 20px;\n  }\n\n  .title-block {\n    flex: 1 1 65%;\n  }\n\n  .title-block h1 {\n    font-size: 22px;\n    font-weight: bold;\n    line-height: 1.25;\n    margin: 0 0 20px 0;\n  }\n\n  .authors {\n    font-size: 14px;\n    margin-top: 10px;\n  }\n\n  .sidebar {\n    flex: 0 0 30%;\n    font-size: 10.5px;\n    color: #222;\n    line-height: 1.5;\n    text-align: left;\n  }\n\n  .sidebar .journal-name {\n    font-weight: bold;\n    font-size: 12px;\n    margin-bottom: 4px;\n  }\n\n  .sidebar .issue-info {\n    margin-bottom: 10px;\n  }\n\n  .sidebar .copyright {\n    margin-bottom: 10px;\n  }\n\n  .sidebar .links a {\n    color: #1a1a1a;\n    text-decoration: none;\n    display: block;\n  }\n\n  .sidebar .doi {\n    margin-top: 4px;\n  }\n\n  .sage-logo {\n    margin-top: 12px;\n    font-weight: bold;\n    font-size: 13px;\n    letter-spacing: 1px;\n  }\n\n  .sage-logo .circle {\n    display: inline-block;\n    width: 10px;\n    height: 10px;\n    border: 1.5px solid #000;\n    border-radius: 50%;\n    margin-right: 4px;\n  }\n\n  .abstract-section {\n    margin-top: 10px;\n  }\n\n  .abstract-section h2 {\n    font-size: 14px;\n    font-weight: bold;\n    margin: 0 0 10px 0;\n  }\n\n  .abstract-section p {\n    font-size: 12.5px;\n    text-align: justify;\n    margin: 0 0 15px 0;\n  }\n\n  .keywords {\n    font-size: 12.5px;\n    margin-bottom: 15px;\n  }\n\n  .keywords .label {\n    font-weight: bold;\n    font-style: italic;\n  }\n\n  .received-info {\n    font-size: 12px;\n    color: #333;\n  }\n\n  em {\n    font-style: italic;\n  }\n\n  sup {\n    font-size: 9px;\n  }\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n\n  <div class=\"article-label\">Article</div>\n\n  <div class=\"header-row\">\n    <div class=\"title-block\">\n      <h1>Can You Tell That I'm in a Relationship?<br>\n      Attachment and Relationship Visibility<br>\n      on Facebook</h1>\n      <div class=\"authors\">\n        Lydia F. Emery<sup>1,2</sup>, Amy Muise<sup>3</sup>, Emily L. Dix<sup>1,4</sup>,<br>\n        and Benjamin Le<sup>1</sup>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"sidebar\">\n      <div class=\"journal-name\">Personality and Social<br>Psychology Bulletin</div>\n      <div class=\"issue-info\">1&ndash;14<br>\n      &copy; 2014 by the Society for Personality<br>\n      and Social Psychology, Inc<br>\n      Reprints and permissions:<br>\n      sagepub.com/journalsPermissions.nav<br>\n      DOI: 10.1177/0146167214549944<br>\n      pspb.sagepub.com</div>\n      <div class=\"sage-logo\">&#9679;SAGE</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"abstract-section\">\n    <h2>Abstract</h2>\n    <p>\n      People often attempt to shape others' perceptions of them, but the role of romantic relationships in this process is unknown.\n      The present set of studies investigates <em>relationship visibility</em>, the centrality of relationships in the self-images that people convey\n      to others. We propose that attachment underlies relationship visibility and test this hypothesis across three studies in the\n      context of Facebook. Avoidant individuals showed low desire for relationship visibility, whereas anxious individuals reported\n      high desired visibility (Studies 1 and 2); however, similar motives drove both groups' actual relationship visibility (Study 1).\n      Moreover, both avoidant individuals and their partners were less likely to make their relationships visible (Studies 1 and 3).\n      On a daily basis, when people felt more insecure about their partner's feelings, they tended to make their relationships visible\n      (Study 3). These studies highlight the role of relationships in how people portray themselves to others.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"keywords\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Keywords</span><br>\n    attachment, close relationships, impression management, Facebook\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"received-info\">\n    Received July 6, 2013; revision accepted July 31, 2014\n  </div>\n\n</body>\n</html>"
